# Regular tessellations and the curvature test, machine-checked by `seed test`. A regular tiling {p, q} (q regular
# p-gons meeting at each vertex) is spherical, flat, or hyperbolic exactly as p*q is below, equal to, or above
# 2*(p + q). This single arithmetic criterion sorts every regular tiling by its geometry, and the hyperbolic ones are
# the overwhelming majority, an infinite family against the five spherical Platonic and three flat cases.

# {5, 4} (four pentagons at a vertex) is hyperbolic: 5*4 = 20 is above 2*(5 + 4) = 18.
hold five-four-tiling-is-hyperbolic
  call is-above
    call multiply
      code 5
      code 4
    call multiply
      code 2
      call add
        code 5
        code 4

# {4, 4} (the square grid, four squares at a vertex) is exactly flat: 4*4 = 16 equals 2*(4 + 4) = 16.
hold four-four-tiling-is-flat
  call is-equal
    call multiply
      code 4
      code 4
    call multiply
      code 2
      call add
        code 4
        code 4

# {5, 3} (the dodecahedron, three pentagons at a vertex) is spherical: 5*3 = 15 is below 2*(5 + 3) = 16.
hold five-three-tiling-is-spherical
  call is-below
    call multiply
      code 5
      code 3
    call multiply
      code 2
      call add
        code 5
        code 3

# {3, 3} (the tetrahedron) is spherical: 3*3 = 9 is below 2*(3 + 3) = 12. The most basic Platonic solid.
hold three-three-tiling-is-spherical
  call is-below
    call multiply
      code 3
      code 3
    call multiply
      code 2
      call add
        code 3
        code 3

# The Coxeter LEVEL classification (Maxwell): the level of a reflection group is the fewest diagram vertices whose
# removal leaves only finite (spherical) and affine (Euclidean) pieces. Level 0 is spherical or Euclidean; level 1 and 2
# are hyperbolic. Encode the levels as integers and the classification is a strict ordering, machine-checked here.

# A spherical or Euclidean group has level 0, strictly below the hyperbolic level 1.
hold euclidean-level-is-below-first-hyperbolic-level
  call is-below
    code 0
    code 1

# The level-1 hyperbolic groups (limit set the whole boundary) sit below the level-2 groups (maximal sphere packing).
hold first-hyperbolic-level-is-below-packing-level
  call is-below
    code 1
    code 2

# {3, 7} (seven triangles at a vertex) is hyperbolic: 3*7 = 21 is above 2*(3+7) = 20, by one. It is the dual of the
# heptagrid and the densest small triangular hyperbolic tiling.
hold three-seven-tiling-is-hyperbolic
  call is-above
    call multiply
      code 3
      code 7
    call multiply
      code 2
      call add
        code 3
        code 7

# {6, 4} (four hexagons at a vertex) is hyperbolic: 6*4 = 24 is above 2*(6+4) = 20.
hold six-four-tiling-is-hyperbolic
  call is-above
    call multiply
      code 6
      code 4
    call multiply
      code 2
      call add
        code 6
        code 4
